{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וישא יעקב רגליו", :translit "v'yisa ya'akov raglav", :gloss "and Jacob lifted his feet"} {:he "ושבתי בשלום", :translit "v'shavti b'shalom", :gloss "and I shall return in peace"} {:he "כה תאמר לבית יעכב", :translit "koh tomar l'veit ya'akov", :gloss "thus you shall say to the house of Jacob"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 7, :occurrences ["ושבתי בשלום"]} {:kind :masorah, :count 25, :occurrences ["ושבתי בשלום"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase וישא יעקב רגליו appears in Genesis 29:1, describing Jacob's journey after his dream at Bethel. Jacob's own statement ושבתי בשלום appears in Genesis 28:21 as part of his vow at Bethel. The phrase כה תאמר לבית יעקב appears in Exodus 19:3 at Sinai. Jacob's children were born during his years with Laban; Genesis 29–30 records the births of the tribes.", :sources ["Genesis 29:1" "Genesis 28:21" "Exodus 19:3" "Genesis 29–30"]}} {:quotes [{:he "בז' דרכים ישם לך שלום", :translit "b'zivchei dar'khim yasem lekha shalom", :gloss "in seven ways He will set peace upon you"} {:he "בז' דרכים ינוסו לפניך", :translit "b'zivchei dar'khim yanusu l'fanekha", :gloss "in seven ways they will flee before you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 7, :occurrences ["וישם"]} {:kind :other, :note "Seven tagin on the word וישם correspond to the seven ways of Leviticus 26 and Deuteronomy 28."}], :frame {:text "Leviticus 26 lists blessings including the enemy fleeing in נגעף, and Deuteronomy 28:7 states the Lord will cause enemies to rise against you to be smitten before your face and flee before you in seven ways.", :sources ["Leviticus 26" "Deuteronomy 28:7"]}} {:quotes [{:he "שלום", :translit "shalom", :gloss "peace"} {:he "עשו", :translit "esav", :gloss "Esau"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "שלום", :b "עשו"}], :frame {:text "The directive to initiate peace greetings is found in the teaching of Rabbi Muna in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi in Yerushalmi Berakhot 9:5, 14a, which states one should be first to extend shalom to every person. Jacob's encounter with Esau in Genesis 33:4, where Esau ran to meet Jacob and kissed him, serves as the biblical model for initiating peace even with a potential adversary.", :sources ["Yerushalmi Berakhot 9:5" "Genesis 33:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 7, :occurrences ["ושבתי בשלום"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ושבתי בשלום", :refs ["Genesis 28:21"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 25, :occurrences ["ושבתי בשלום"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ושבתי בשלום", :refs ["Genesis 28:21"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 7, :occurrences ["וישם"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "וישם", :refs ["Genesis 2:8" "Genesis 4:15" "Genesis 22:6" "Genesis 22:9" "Genesis 24:9" "Genesis 28:11" "Genesis 28:18" "Genesis 30:36"], :n 87, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Seven tagin on the word וישם correspond to the seven ways of Leviticus 26 and Deuteronomy 28.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "שלום", :a-value 376, :b "עשו", :b-value 376, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:27:28.509761904Z", :model "glm-5.2"}